Therapist: Hi David, good to see you. How's the past two weeks been since our last session?
Client: Busy. Meridian Logistics rolled out this new routing software and I've basically been the point person for training the whole regional team on it.
Therapist: That sounds like a lot of extra responsibility landed on you at once. How has that been sitting with you?
Client: I'm exhausted, honestly. I've been eating lunch at my desk every day, and most nights I don't stop working until eight or nine.
Therapist: You mentioned "eating lunch at my desk" — has anything changed about your eating more broadly, beyond just the timing?
Client: Yeah, actually. I've noticed I'm eating a lot more takeout, and later at night than usual. Sometimes I don't even really taste it, I'm just eating because I'm stressed, not because I'm hungry.
Therapist: How often would you say that's happening now compared to a couple months ago?
Client: Maybe four or five nights a week now, versus once or twice before. And I've put on some weight because of it, which is its own stress on top of everything else.
Therapist: That sounds like it's becoming its own cycle — stress leading to the eating pattern, and then the weight change adding a new layer of stress back on top.
Client: Exactly. It feels like I can't get out from under it.
Therapist: I want to flag something for later in our session — I think it could genuinely help to bring in some outside support specifically around the eating and stress piece, alongside what we do here. I'll come back to that. First, let's dig into the work stress itself, since that seems to be the engine driving a lot of this. Can you walk me through a specific hard moment from the last two weeks?
Client: Sure. Last Wednesday I had to present the rollout timeline to the regional VP, and halfway through she interrupted and said the timeline was unrealistic in front of the whole team.
Therapist: What went through your mind in that moment?
Client: Something like, "I've completely failed at this, and now everyone on the call thinks I'm incompetent."
Therapist: Let's slow down and actually examine that thought using the thought record we've used before. First — what's the evidence that supports "I've completely failed and everyone thinks I'm incompetent"?
Client: I mean, she said it in front of everyone. That's pretty direct evidence.
Therapist: Okay, that's one piece. Is there anything else supporting it, or is that the main piece of evidence?
Client: Honestly, that's about it when I think about it.
Therapist: Now let's look at evidence against that thought. Has anyone else on the team said anything critical since then?
Client: No, actually. Two people messaged me afterward saying the timeline concern was more about the VP's own deadline pressure from her boss, not about my work specifically.
Therapist: That's important evidence. What did your own manager say afterward?
Client: He said I handled the pushback well and that the concern was fair to raise but not really about me personally.
Therapist: So looking at both sides — the VP's comment on one hand, and your manager's and colleagues' reactions on the other — what's a more balanced thought you could put in place of "I've completely failed and everyone thinks I'm incompetent"?
Client: Maybe something like, "The timeline got pushed back because of pressure above me, and my team didn't actually lose confidence in me."
Therapist: How does that version feel compared to the original one?
Client: A lot lighter, honestly. When I actually look at the evidence instead of just the gut reaction, it's a totally different picture.
Therapist: That gap between the gut reaction and the evidence-based version is exactly what we're training your brain to catch earlier, in the moment, rather than two weeks later in here. Let's actually run through the same process with a second example, since repetition is what makes this start to feel automatic. Was there another moment recently where a similar spiral happened?
Client: Yeah, actually — Friday, one of the drivers messaged the group chat saying the new routing software sent him to the wrong warehouse, and my first thought was, "I built this training wrong and now the whole rollout is going to fail because of me."
Therapist: Same process — what's the evidence for that thought?
Client: One driver had one issue. That's really the only evidence.
Therapist: And against it?
Client: Twenty-something other drivers didn't report any issues. And when I looked into it, the routing software had the address logged wrong for that one warehouse — it wasn't even related to the training.
Therapist: So what's the more balanced thought, looking at both sides?
Client: Something like, "One data error caused one driver's issue, and it wasn't actually about my training at all."
Therapist: How quickly were you able to get to that balanced version this time, compared to the VP example a minute ago?
Client: Actually pretty fast, now that I've done it twice in a row. I can see the pattern more clearly the second time through.
Therapist: That's exactly the kind of skill-building we're going for — each time you deliberately walk through it, the next one gets a little faster and more automatic. I want to build on that with something practical, since a lot of this workload piling up seems tied to how hard it is for you to push back when more gets added to your plate. Has anyone asked you to take on something new since the rollout started?
Client: Actually yeah — my manager asked yesterday if I could also take over the vendor onboarding documentation, on top of the training I'm already doing.
Therapist: What did you say?
Client: I said yes, obviously, even though I have no idea when I'm supposed to fit that in.
Therapist: What would you have wanted to say, if saying no felt more available to you?
Client: Probably something like, "I can take that on, but something else needs to come off my plate first, or the timeline needs to shift."
Therapist: That's a really solid, reasonable response. Let's actually practice saying that out loud right now, with me playing your manager, so it's less foreign the next time this comes up in the moment.
Client: Okay, that feels a little awkward, but sure.
Therapist: Alright — David, I need you to also take over the vendor onboarding documentation this week.
Client: I can take that on, but something else would need to come off my plate first, or we'd need to shift the timeline. Realistically I can't do both well at the same time.
Therapist: How did it feel to say that, even in a practice version?
Client: Weirdly hard, honestly, even knowing it wasn't real. My instinct is so strongly just to say yes and figure it out later.
Therapist: That instinct makes sense given how you've operated for a long time, but you can see how it's part of what's feeding the exhaustion and the eating pattern we talked about earlier. I'd like you to actually use a version of that line for real this week, the next time something new gets added.
Client: I can try. It'll probably feel just as awkward in the moment as it did just now.
Therapist: That's completely normal the first few times — the goal isn't for it to feel comfortable yet, just for you to actually say it. I want to shift now to something a little different — a values exercise, since a lot of what's driving the late nights isn't just the workload itself, it's also about what you think you're supposed to be sacrificing for it.
Client: Yeah, that's fair. Alex has been pretty patient about the hours, but I can tell it's wearing on both of us.
Therapist: Let's actually name that directly. If you imagine your ideal week, one where work and everything else felt properly balanced, what would matter most to you in it?
Client: Having dinner with Alex most nights, actually being present instead of half-checking my phone. And I used to run three times a week and I haven't done that in probably two months.
Therapist: Those are two clear values — connection with your partner, and taking care of your physical health. Right now, how much of your actual week reflects those two things?
Client: Almost none of it, if I'm honest. Dinner's usually me eating at my desk, and running is the first thing that gets cut when I'm behind.
Therapist: So there's a real gap between what you value and how you're actually spending your time right now. That gap is often exactly what produces the exhaustion you're describing — it's not just the hours themselves, it's the sense that the hours are coming at the cost of things that matter to you.
Client: That's a really good way to put it. I hadn't thought about it as a values gap, just as "there's too much work."
Therapist: Both things can be true at once — the workload is genuinely heavy, and also, some choices about how you're responding to it are widening that values gap more than necessary. What's one small, concrete way you could close that gap even slightly this week?
Client: Maybe protecting Tuesday and Thursday dinners specifically, even if I have to log back on after. And I could probably fit in one run this week, even a short one.
Therapist: Those both sound realistic and specific. I'd like you to commit to those two things — protected dinner Tuesday and Thursday, and one run — and we'll check in on how that landed next time.
Client: Okay, I can do that. Writing it down now actually.
Therapist: Good. Now, coming back to the eating pattern we talked about earlier — I'd like to make a referral to Dr. Priya Nair, a nutritionist who works closely with a few of my other clients dealing with stress-related eating changes. She takes a practical, non-judgmental approach, which I think would suit you well here. Her office number is 555-0172. How does that sound?
Client: Honestly, I think that would help. I don't really know how to think about the eating piece on my own, and it feels separate from the work stress conversation even though I know it's connected.
Therapist: It's very connected, but it also deserves its own focused attention, which is exactly what she can provide alongside what we're doing here. I'll send the referral over today, and you can expect her office to reach out within a few days to schedule.
Client: That works. I'll keep an eye out for the call.
Therapist: One last thing before we close — how has your sleep been through all of this? We haven't checked in on that specifically in a while.
Client: Not great. I'd say six hours most nights, and I'm usually on my laptop right up until I turn the light off.
Therapist: That's worth flagging alongside everything else, since poor sleep tends to make both the anxiety around work and the stress eating harder to manage. Walk me through what actually happens between closing the laptop and falling asleep right now.
Client: Honestly there isn't much of a gap. I close it, brush my teeth, and get straight into bed still kind of wired from whatever I was just working on.
Therapist: That makes sense why falling asleep might be harder than it needs to be — your mind hasn't had any transition time between work mode and rest mode. Would you be willing to add a small boundary there too — laptop closed by a specific time, even fifteen minutes earlier than you're doing now, with something calming in between?
Client: I could try nine-thirty instead of whenever I actually stop, which is usually closer to ten or ten-thirty. Maybe read something that isn't work-related in that gap.
Therapist: That's a reasonable, small step, and it pairs well with everything else we set up today. To summarize today — we reframed both the VP presentation and the driver routing issue using the thought record, practiced a boundary-setting script for when new work gets added, identified a real gap between your values around connection and health and how your week is actually structured, committed to two protected dinners and a run this week, agreed to close the laptop by nine-thirty with some wind-down reading, and I'm sending a referral to Dr. Nair for the eating and stress piece.
Client: That's a lot, but it all makes sense together. I feel like I actually have a plan instead of just feeling drowned by everything.
Therapist: That's exactly the shift I want to see. Let's plan to check in on all of it — both reframes, the boundary script, the dinners and run, the laptop cutoff, and the referral — next time.
Client: Sounds good. Same time next week?
Therapist: Same time next week. Take care, David.
Client: Thanks, you too.
